>> title: Chintan Broth, Instant Pot
>> category: Soups
>> tags: chicken, pork, japanese, stock, ramen, instant pot, pressure cooker
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> aka: instant pot chintan, pressure cooker ramen broth, pressure cooker chintan, clear ramen broth, torigara broth, 清湯, the broth under shoyu and shio ramen
>> servings: 8
>> dish: chintan-broth
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> pairs-with: shoyu-ramen, shio-ramen, miso-ramen
>> time: 3 hr
>> step.1: Clear is the one thing a sealed pot is worst at. This trades some of it for four hours.
>> step.2: parboil 10 min, rinse every piece
>> step.3: cook at high pressure 60 min
>> step.4: wait out the full natural release 30 min — never the valve
>> step.5: steep the aromatics and kombu 30 min in the residual heat
>> step.6: strain through cloth, stand 20 min, lift the fat off and keep it

The stovetop version is four hours of never letting it boil, and clarity is the whole point of it. A pressure cooker holds the liquid above boiling and moves it, and you cannot see in to skim, so be honest about what this is: a broth that is gold and clean-tasting but not glass. It is worth it about four nights in five. When you want the one you can read a newspaper through, use the pot on the stove.

Cover @chicken carcasses{2 1/2%lb}(1.1 kg; backs and frames, wing tips left on), @chicken wings{1%lb}(450 g) and @pork neck bones{3/4%lb}(340 g) with @cold water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in the #Instant Pot{}, bring up to a boil on Sauté and ~parboil{10%min}, then drain and rinse every piece under the tap and scrub the inner pot. This is the only skimming you get, so take the time.

Return @&(~1)rinsed bones{} to the pot with @water{2 1/2%qt}(2.4 L), lock the lid and ~pressure cook{60%min} at high pressure. Two and a half quarts against the stovetop five: nothing evaporates in here, and the pot wants to be no more than two thirds full.

Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{30%min} until the pin drops by itself. Do not touch the valve. Opening it drops the pressure at a stroke, the liquid flashes to a violent boil inside the pot, and every bit of fat you were about to lift off gets beaten into the broth instead — which is exactly the failure the stovetop version spends four hours avoiding.

Drop @yellow onion{1%large}(halved), @fresh ginger{2%in}(sliced), @scallion greens{4%stalks} and @kombu{1%piece}(4 in) into @&(~1)broth{} and ~steep{30%min} in the residual heat with the lid resting on top. They never see pressure: an hour sealed takes onion to nothing and turns kombu slippery, and the pot is still near boiling when it opens, which is all this needs.

Strain @&(~1)broth{} through a fine sieve lined with a cloth, let it ~stand{20%min} and lift the fat off the top — or run it through a fat separator, which does the same job at once. That fat is the chicken fat the shoyu and shio bowls float on and belongs in a jar. Chill the strained broth if you have the night; the cap sets firmer and lifts cleaner, and it is the only part of the stovetop method worth keeping.
